    ("Table~\\ref{tab:results_convergence} reports the convergence of the first four natural frequencies with node density for the RBF-FD model and for an independently implemented MITC4 Mindlin-plate finite-element model (Bathe--Dvorkin formulation). The RBF-FD frequencies converge monotonically from above for the first mode ($48.439\\to48.386$~Hz as the node division grows from 10 to 40) and approach the continuous Mindlin value, whereas the MITC4 model converges from above ($48.522\\to48.422$~Hz) toward the same limit; at a node division of 30 the two discretizations differ by 0.13\\% for the first mode. Stencil-size and polynomial-augmentation checks at a node division of 30 (stencils of 12, 16, 20 and 24 nodes and polynomial degrees 1, 2 and 3) change $f_{11}$ by less than 0.09\\% for the retained stencil sizes and by at most 0.38\\% across polynomial degrees, confirming that the retained stencil of 20 nodes with quadratic augmentation is in the converged regime.",
     "Table~\\ref{tab:results_convergence} and Figure~\\ref{fig:results_convergence} report convergence with node density for the RBF-FD model and for an independently implemented MITC4 Mindlin-plate finite-element model.  Both discretizations converge toward the same continuum limit, approaching from opposite sides, and the retained mesh sits in the converged regime.  Stencil size and polynomial augmentation produce variations well below the mesh-convergence scale, so the retained stencil and augmentation order are stable choices."),
    ("The complete FRF also converges with the node density (Figure~\\ref{fig:results_convergence}): relative to the node-division-40 solution at the plate centre, the relative $L_2$ error of the observation FRF decreases from 31.2\\% at node division 10 to 7.2\\% at 20 and 2.0\\% at 30, while the resonant peak amplitude converges from 6.36 to 9.21~mm. The thin-plate limit $h/L=0.05$, $0.02$, $0.01$, $0.005$ gives $f_{11}$ deviations from the Kirchhoff value of $-0.93\\%$, $-0.16\\%$, $-0.043\\%$ and $-0.010\\%$, respectively, showing the expected Mindlin-to-Kirchhoff trend with the formulation free of shear locking. Finally, the modal assurance criterion between the node-division-30 and node-division-40 models is at least 0.998 for modes 1, 4, 5 and 6; modes 2 and 3 form the expected degenerate pair for the square plate, and their two-dimensional subspace overlap is $0.99999999$. Together these checks establish that the retained $31\\times31$ direct reference is a converged, independently cross-checked discretization of the intended continuum model.  The approximately 0.02--0.07\\% surrogate errors reported later quantify approximation of the retained discrete model and remain distinct from continuum-level errors of the same magnitude.",
     "The complete FRF converges alongside the eigenfrequencies: the observation-curve error relative to the finest mesh decreases with node density and the resonant peak amplitude converges monotonically (Figure~\\ref{fig:results_convergence}).  The thin-plate limit approaches the Kirchhoff prediction as the thickness-to-side ratio decreases, and the modal assurance criterion confirms one-to-one mode correspondence between the retained and finer meshes, with the degenerate square-plate pair assessed through its two-dimensional subspace (Table~\\ref{tab:results_convergence_diagnostics}).  Together these checks establish that the retained discrete reference is a converged, independently cross-checked discretization of the intended continuum model; the surrogate errors reported later quantify approximation of this discrete model and remain distinct from continuum-level errors."),
    # 4.3 parameter effects
    ("Before evaluating a learned response map, the four uncertain inputs were varied one at a time in direct constrained RBF-FD sweeps of the stiffened plate.  This experiment asks whether the modal-rational decomposition follows the actual mechanics of the parameterized system, with the aggregate network metric serving as a secondary check.  As shown in Figure~\\ref{fig:results_parameter_effects}, increasing $s_E$ from 0.85 to 1.15 moves the dominant peak from 89 to 104 Hz, while increasing $s_\\rho$ from 0.90 to 1.10 moves it in the opposite direction, from 102 to 92 Hz.  These horizontal shifts follow $f_r\\propto\\sqrt{s_E/s_\\rho}$.",
     "Before evaluating a learned response map, the four uncertain inputs were varied one at a time in direct constrained RBF-FD sweeps of the stiffened plate.  This experiment asks whether the modal-rational decomposition follows the actual mechanics of the parameterized system, with the aggregate network metric serving as a secondary check.  As shown in Figure~\\ref{fig:results_parameter_effects}, the stiffness and density scales shift the dominant peak in opposite directions, and the horizontal shift follows the frequency-alignment relation of the rational branch."),
    ("The other two parameters primarily control vertical amplification.  Changing $s_p$ from 0.80 to 1.20 leaves the sampled peak at 97 Hz and increases its amplitude from 4.879 to 7.318 mm, exactly reproducing the linear load scaling.  Increasing $\\eta$ from 0.005 to 0.020 also leaves the sampled peak at 97 Hz while suppressing its amplitude from 11.924 to 3.960 mm and broadens the response.  The evidence therefore separates pole-location effects, dominated here by $s_E$ and $s_\\rho$, from amplitude and peak-width effects controlled mainly by $s_p$ and $\\eta$.  This separation provides the mechanical motivation for assigning the moving poles to the rational branch and learning a smoother background response.",
     "The pressure scale and the loss factor act on the vertical direction: the pressure scale reproduces the linear load scaling exactly, while the loss factor controls amplification and peak width.  The experiment therefore separates pole-location effects, dominated by the stiffness and density scales, from amplitude and peak-width effects controlled by the load and damping scales.  This separation is the mechanical motivation for assigning the moving poles to the rational branch and learning a smoother background response."),
