    # 4.5 classical baselines
    ("The surrogate is benchmarked against the classical methods that a reviewer would reasonably apply to this parameter family: (i) the pole-only rational branch with the production mode count (four modes for the unstiffened plate, one for the stiffened plate); (ii) modal superposition with 8 and 16 modes (ms8/ms16); (iii) mode acceleration with static correction (ma\\_sc4/ma\\_sc8); (iv) a nearest-calibration-curve baseline after exact frequency alignment $f\\sqrt{s_\\rho/s_E}$ and amplitude normalization $us_E/s_p$ (nn\\_aligned) and its linear interpolation in the loss factor (interp\\_aligned); and (v) the pole-free POD-MLP ablation variant (pod\\_mlp, 1500 epochs $\\times$ 3 members). All methods are evaluated on the same 40 independent test realizations and the same 201-point frequency grid as the proposed surrogate.",
     "The surrogate is benchmarked against the classical methods that a reviewer would reasonably apply to this parameter family: the pole-only rational branch with the production mode count, modal superposition with 8 and 16 modes, mode acceleration with static correction (ma\\_sc4/ma\\_sc8), frequency-aligned nearest-curve and interpolation baselines, and the pole-free POD-MLP variant.  All methods are evaluated on the same 40 independent test realizations and the same 201-point frequency grid as the proposed surrogate."),
    ("Table~\\ref{tab:results_classical_baselines} summarizes the observation-FRF relative $L_2$ errors. The pole-only branch already achieves median errors of 0.767\\% (unstiffened) and 0.203\\% (stiffened), reproducing the diagnostic that the exact rational branch is the dominant mechanism. Enriching the modal basis reduces the error to 0.091\\%/0.073\\% (ms8) and 0.074\\% (ms16; only eight modes are active below 800 Hz in the stiffened case). Mode acceleration with static correction reaches 0.071\\%/0.023\\% (ma\\_sc8), the strongest classical baseline. The frequency-aligned interpolation baselines remain above 11\\% median error, largely because the 1 Hz calibration grid undersamples the sharply resonant curves and the interpolated pole amplitude near $\\eta$ inherits the coarse-grid resolution; they are reported for completeness. The pole-free POD-MLP reaches only 71.8\\%/50.2\\%, confirming that a smooth neural background requires the rational branch to represent the resonance-dominated family. The proposed surrogate (0.0708\\%/0.0210\\%) is statistically indistinguishable in order of magnitude from ma\\_sc8 on the observation FRF. The proportional parameter family considered here is close to an ideal operating regime for classical modal reduction: the modal basis is fixed, pole migration is analytically tractable and static correction efficiently captures the truncated contribution. The observation that mode acceleration with static correction matches the proposed surrogate therefore defines the appropriate classical reference limit that a hybrid representation should recover before it is extended to parameter families for which the modal subspace itself changes. For the present benchmark, if only an observation FRF is required, the classical modal method should be preferred. The distinguishing feature of the surrogate is the integration of a full-field response---available from modal superposition as well---with a learned residual representation, the same discrete equilibrium interface, and the subsequent calibration and UQ workflow.  To quantify the trade-off when the task is extended from a single observation FRF to the full ellipsoidal UQ workflow, Table~\\ref{tab:results_ma_sc8_uq} compares ma\\_sc8 with the surrogate over the same 1000 ellipsoidal realizations.  The two methods produce nearly identical envelopes (relative $L_2$ differences of 1.2\\% for the lower and 0.10\\% for the upper envelope of the unstiffened plate, and below 0.08\\% for the stiffened plate), resonance-frequency ranges and peak-amplitude ranges.  For observation-only envelopes the classical baseline is therefore the preferred tool in the present proportional benchmark, while the surrogate additionally supplies the calibrated band, the full-field workflow and a path toward parameter families that require a generalized modal representation.",
     "Table~\\ref{tab:results_classical_baselines} and Figure~\\ref{fig:results_classical_baselines} summarize the observation-FRF errors.  The pole-only branch is already accurate, confirming that the exact rational branch carries the dominant resonant mechanism; enriching the modal basis and adding static correction bring the classical methods to the accuracy level of the proposed surrogate, while the frequency-aligned interpolation baselines and the pole-free POD-MLP remain substantially less accurate.  The proportional parameter family considered here is close to an ideal operating regime for classical modal reduction: the modal basis is fixed, pole migration is analytically tractable and static correction efficiently captures the truncated contribution.  The observation that mode acceleration with static correction matches the proposed surrogate therefore defines the appropriate classical reference limit that a hybrid representation should recover before it is extended to parameter families for which the modal subspace itself changes.  For the present benchmark, if only an observation FRF is required, the classical modal method should be preferred.  The distinguishing feature of the surrogate is the integration of a full-field response---available from modal superposition as well---with a learned residual representation, the same discrete equilibrium interface, and the subsequent calibration and UQ workflow.  When the task is extended to the complete ellipsoidal UQ workflow, Table~\\ref{tab:results_ma_sc8_uq} shows that the two methods produce nearly identical envelopes, resonance-frequency ranges and peak-amplitude ranges over 1000 realizations; the classical baseline is again the economical choice for observation-only envelopes, while the surrogate additionally supplies the calibrated band, the full-field workflow and a path toward parameter families that require a generalized modal representation."),
    ("The online cost of the classical baselines is negligible: one 201-point query takes about $1.1\\times10^{-5}$--$2.2\\times10^{-5}$~s for pole-only, ms16 and ma\\_sc8 on the same hardware, and their offline cost is only 2.4--3.1~s (operator assembly, a 16-mode eigensolve and one static-correction solve). The direct RBF-FD query requires 257.8~s (unstiffened) and 331.7~s (stiffened), while the proposed surrogate requires 0.0020 and 0.0026~s. Section~\\ref{subsec:results_cost} re-evaluates the break-even comparison including these classical baselines.",
     "The online cost of the classical baselines is negligible, and their offline preparation (operator assembly, a 16-mode eigensolve and one static-correction solve) is far smaller than the surrogate construction; the direct RBF-FD query is orders of magnitude slower than both.  Section~\\ref{subsec:results_cost} re-evaluates the break-even comparison including these classical baselines."),
